{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>Parsing JSON out of a paragraph the model wrote around it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You ask an LLM to return JSON describing a UI card: a title, an icon name, a color. It replies with "Sure! Here's the JSON you asked for:" followed by the actual object, then a closing sentence explaining what it did.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your code does <code>JSON.parse(response)</code> and immediately throws, because the response isn't JSON, it's a sentence with JSON somewhere inside it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Regex-stripping the surrounding text works until the model changes its phrasing, wraps the JSON in a markdown code fence, or adds a trailing note. Every one of those is a silent parse failure in production.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Structured output modes solve this at the API level: instead of asking the model to produce JSON and hoping it complies with the formatting, you give the API a schema and it constrains the model's output to match that schema exactly, no prose, no parsing gymnastics.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>undefined</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Think of it like a form with fields the model has to fill in, instead of a blank page where it might write anything. You describe the shape you want, and the response comes back already in that shape.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      You pass a JSON schema describing the exact fields, types, and structure you need. The API guarantees the response is valid JSON matching that schema, so you can call <code>JSON.parse()</code> directly and trust the result without a validation-and-retry loop.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is enforced at generation time, not checked after the fact: the model is constrained token-by-token to only produce output that can complete into a schema-valid document, which is a stronger guarantee than prompting the model to "only output JSON" and then validating the response yourself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The dashboard widget generator that broke on one model update</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team built a feature where an LLM generates dashboard widget configs, JSON describing chart type, data source, and title, from a plain-English request. They extracted JSON from the response with a regex that looked for the first <code>{</code> and the last <code>}</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      It worked for months. Then a model update changed the response format slightly, adding a trailing explanation after the JSON, and the regex started capturing extra text past the closing brace, breaking the parse for every request.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Switching to structured output with an explicit schema removed the regex entirely. The API response was always valid, parseable JSON, with no dependency on the model's incidental formatting choices.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This also let the team tighten their schema over time, for example, constraining the chart type field to an enum of only the chart components the dashboard actually supports, so the model literally cannot request card unsupported chart type. Invalid requests became structurally impossible rather than something to catch after the fact.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

