{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Schema design as a product decision, not an implementation detail</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Keep your schema close to what your UI actually needs to render. If a field's value can only ever be one of a few options, use an enum for it instead of a free-text string; that way the model can't return something your UI doesn't know how to handle.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Version your schemas the same way you'd version an API contract. If you add a required field, existing cached or logged responses generated under the old schema won't have it, so plan a migration path rather than assuming every response your system has ever produced matches today's schema.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Structured output narrows what the model can say, but it doesn't guarantee the values are correct, only that they're shaped correctly. A schema-valid response can still contain a hallucinated data source name or a nonsensical title; treat schema conformance as a parsing guarantee, not a correctness guarantee, and validate semantic constraints separately.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

